    "justification": "The endpoint result proves exactly when finite component classification can be charged to noncoverage rather than to expected diameter.",
    "gap": "EvansHansenStark2005 gives general confidence-set decision duality, but not this nuisance-profiled four-line panel experiment, its explicit finite-\\(T_0\\) error threshold, or its post-noise-only endpoint width.",
    "consumer": "It gives a transparent diagnostic for when a certified maximal loading margin makes margin-aware intervals much shorter than globally honest intervals.",
    "proof_tex": "The proved \\texttt{lem:four-line-classification} gives \\(\\kappa_\\star=1/\\sqrt3\\), identifies the endpoint stratum with the four lines \\(P_h\\), and gives\n\\[\n \\sup_{\\vartheta\\in\\mathcal M_{T_0,T_1}(1/\\sqrt3)}\n \\Pr_\\vartheta(\\widehat h\\ne h)\\le\\alpha/2\n\\]\nwhenever \\(T_0\\ge\\overline T\\).  Conditional on the true label \\(h\\), the proved \\texttt{lem:post-mean-law} gives \\(z=m+\\beta+\\eta\\), with \\(m\\in\\operatorname{range}(P_h)\\), and the definition of \\(\\varepsilon_1\\) gives \\(\\Pr(\\lVert\\eta\\rVert_2>\\varepsilon_1)\\le\\alpha/2\\).  On the intersection of correct classification and the post-noise event, the true \\(m,\\beta\\) witness feasibility in \\texttt{def:endpoint-confidence-set}.  The union bound therefore gives\n\\[\n \\inf_{\\vartheta\\in\\mathcal M_{T_0,T_1}(1/\\sqrt3)}\n \\Pr_\\vartheta\\{\\beta\\in\\widehat{\\mathcal C}_{\\mathrm{end}}\\}\n \\ge1-\\alpha.\n\\]\nThe compactness and measurability argument from the proved \\texttt{thm:global-honesty} applies verbatim after replacing the compact loading region by the measurable selected projector \\(P_{\\widehat h}\\).\n\nFor a pathwise diameter bound, take any two accepted vectors \\(b_0,b_1\\), with witnesses \\(m_0,m_1\\in\\operatorname{range}(P_{\\widehat h})\\) and residuals \\(r_j=z-m_j-b_j\\).  Then\n\\[\n (I_3-P_{\\widehat h})(b_0-b_1)\n =(I_3-P_{\\widehat h})(r_1-r_0).\n\\]\nThe difference \\(b_0-b_1\\) is at most two-sparse, and every selected line has margin \\(1/\\sqrt3\\).  Hence\n\\[\n \\lVert b_0-b_1\\rVert_2\\le2\\sqrt3\\,\\varepsilon_1.\n\\]\nIf the supports agree, the cube gives distance at most \\(2B\\); if they differ, it gives at most \\(\\sqrt2B\\le2B\\).  Therefore, for every realization and every selected label, including a wrong one,\n\\[\n \\operatorname{diam}_2(\\widehat{\\mathcal C}_{\\mathrm{end}})\n \\le\\min\\{2B,2\\sqrt3\\varepsilon_1\\}.\n\\]\nThis explains why classification error consumes coverage probability but creates no expected-diameter remainder.\n\nThe last display and \\(\\varepsilon_1\\asymp T_1^{-1/2}\\) give\n\\[\n R_{\\mathrm{or}}(1/\\sqrt3)\\le C\\min\\{B,T_1^{-1/2}\\}.\n\\]\nFor the reverse inequality, fix any endpoint line and legal pre parameters, use constant post factors with \\(m=0\\), and compare \\(\\beta_0=0\\) with \\(\\beta_1=t e_j\\), where \\(t=c\\min\\{B,T_1^{-1/2}\\}\\).  Both laws remain in the endpoint stratum, their pre laws coincide, and their full post Gaussian mean arrays differ by \\(\\sqrt{T_1}t\\).  The elementary two-point and Gaussian total-variation calculation in the proof of \\texttt{thm:adaptive-frontier} gives\n\\[\n R_{\\mathrm{or}}(1/\\sqrt3)\\ge c'\\min\\{B,T_1^{-1/2}\\}.\n\\]\nFinally, the proved \\texttt{thm:adaptive-frontier} evaluated at \\(\\gamma=1/\\sqrt3\\) gives \\(R_{\\mathrm{ad}}(1/\\sqrt3)\\asymp\\rho_{\\mathrm{ad}}(1/\\sqrt3)\\).  This proves all conclusions uniformly in \\(T_1\\ge1\\) under the stated threshold on \\(T_0\\).",
